  {
    "episode_id": 77032,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77032.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "it's court of my existence michael i uh... lost my dad when i six months old in the vietnam war captain already pepper he grew up uh... very poor in st pal my grandfather died when he was just a little boy my grandmother wrote raised two boys, my uncle Howard and my dad already by working in the men's clothing department at a department store in St. Paul, Minnesota. My dad couldn't afford college, so he took an ROTC scholarship, graduated from the University of Minnesota Law School, magna cum laude, actually just cum laude, sorry, I didn't want to give him too much credit. And he then was sent to Vietnam to serve his country. was a captain and was killed in a helicopter crash and played cool in July of 1969 when I was just six months old and it very much shaped my life because with his loss my mom was 24 and widowed we had to live with my great grandparents for the first two years of my life and then in an act of extraordinary good fortune I was adopted by my dad Eddie into a remarkable family of many blessings My adopted grandma was the advice columnist, Dear Abby, and my aunt, Ann Landers, so a lot of advice in my upbringing. But most importantly, just this year has been a remarkable year for me, Mike Leit. For the first time in my life in H-54, went to Vietnam for the first time, and went to the very site on Dragon Mountain in Placo, Vietnam, where my dad died. And I got to stand there with others and celebrate him and the other seven soldiers that died that day. I literally took some of that dirt back home with me and in no small part has influenced my decision to serve my country at a time I believe we needed more than ever for reasons I'm sure many listeners will understand.",
