  {
    "episode_id": 77032,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77032.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "Well, first of all, I'm one of you. For most of my life, I thought the same things. I was just as appalled, disgusted, dismayed. Finally, the morning after the 2016 election, I had to tell everybody. I watched it that night with my family. I woke up the next morning. My daughters were in tears. My youngest at home in Minneapolis, my eldest freshman in college, who had voted for the first time at age 18. Via FaceTime, I sat at the breakfast table With them, I promised them I would do something. I'd raise them not to be observers, to be participants. I'd taken our country, our governance, and those who populate it, totally for granted. And I had to activate. And it was integral to their future, to my children's, to everybody's who's listening right now. And to all the independents who might be listening right now, you know, I'm one of you. You have to be a Democrat or Republican to serve in Congress. We have a duopoly of political industrial complex. I'm a proud Democrat. I'm a Humphrey Democrat, probably no greater hero to me than him. But I'm speaking for what I call the exhausted majority of people of decency, of integrity, perhaps physically responsible, but socially reasonable, who care deeply about our country, about our future, and are just appalled by two parties who are pulling us drastically to the far right, to the far left. I say to many, Michael, that I believe we don't have a hardware problem in America. We have a software problem. Our system is the most remarkable in human history, self-governance, but we are populating our legislatures, Congress, many of our state houses, with people who are simply lacking the intention to do the jobs that we voters expect and demand. And that's my voice. I believe Joe Biden is a good man. I respect him, but it is time for change. There's a massive, massive disconnect between the Washington industrial political complex and the rest of the country. I've only been on the road now for three days in New Hampshire, and let me tell you, Michael, It is a world of difference from what those on Twitter, from what those watching Evening Cable News, those in the political world, what they think is really happening, massive disconnect. So I'm speaking for everybody who's sick of the nonsense, who recognizes that politics is disastrous for all of us.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "There is a lot happening right now.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "I am still trying to determine my own position on the matter.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "I am unsure how useful my input will be.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "I will attempt to share my thoughts despite uncertainty.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Sharing uncertain thoughts is still valuable or worthwhile.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener expects some form of contribution even when the speaker is unclear.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Uncertainty does not preclude meaningful communication.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
