  {
    "episode_id": 77032,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77032.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "Well, that's the entry. That's the door opener, Michael. With all that said, we have a good macroeconomy that the president likes to talk about. Our microeconomy, the way that people are struggling right now, is extraordinarily important for people to recognize. We have got to lower costs. America is way too expensive. Housing prices going up drastically. Food and fuel way too expensive. People can't afford childcare. Healthcare is inaccessible. We pay twice as much than any country in the entire world. And the very pharmaceuticals invented here made here oftentimes finance with US taxpayer dollars are sold to us Americans for two, three, four, five times more than anyone else in the world. I'm going to attack that. We've got chaos at the border. We've got chaos in our cities. I want to use AI, which of course has threats, which we will manage and regulate. I want to use AI to fundamentally change how we apply the United States federal government. We will save money for the government. We will save money for consumers. I want to comprehensively reform the structure of the federal government to the extent the United States President can. I want to ensure that a cabinet is populated with the best and brightest, regardless of their politics, if their principles and character are strong. I believe in zero-based budgeting. We should not be simply adding more layers of money every single year to the U.S. federal budget. I believe in term limits. We need more turnover, both in Congress and in our agencies. I believe in term limits for the Supreme Court. I believe we've got to take better care of our planet. We've got to stop pollution. Gun violence out of control. I'm in New Hampshire, 18 people massacred in Maine right next door. I'm a gun owner. I know how to bring people together in a way that I think no former president in my lifetime has ever even tried. And as the most bipartisan member of Congress, I think I was number one last year, number two this year out of 535 members. I have great relationships with my Republican colleagues who frankly want to achieve the same objectives. And I want to ask Americans to give me a shot. As your president, over four years, I will fundamentally change how that job is executed in the executive branch by somebody who's only been doing the legislative work for five years, not for 50 years, and turn it over to someone who gets what the country really, really is asking for, which is integrity, good character, executive experience, and some bold plans that are not imposed on Catholic America, but are inspired by common sentences and common ground. That's my pitch.",
